WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.720
As we saw in the previous set of topics,&nbsp;
containment uses the in keyword to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:11.120
determine whether a value is in a list of values.&nbsp;
Containment is important when one needs to know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.280
for example, if an answer someone entered&nbsp;
for a question is in an acceptable list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:23.360
answers. For example, in this 146-containment&nbsp;
file, we have a list of ancient civilizations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:31.240
stored in a variable with that name on line&nbsp;
1. Line 3 has a print statement to see if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:41.000
Egypt is one of the ancient civilizations.&nbsp;
On line 4, Japan is tested, and on line 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:51.320
China is tested. I will run this code, and&nbsp;
we see that Egypt and China return true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:58.800
but Japan returns false, which is to be expected&nbsp;
given the list of ancient civilizations on line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.680
1. In programming, many keywords that determine&nbsp;
a true or false value can have the word not added&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.080
to look for an opposite result. A person may be&nbsp;
asked to enter a territory not part of an ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:22.320
civilization. To test this not keyword, I will add&nbsp;
the word not in front of the in keyword on line 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:29.840
rerun the code, and now for our third&nbsp;
test for China, we see a value of false,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:39.120
as this statement of China not being in the list&nbsp;
of ancient civilizations is indeed false. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:47.320
reached the end of this topic, so it is time for&nbsp;
another review. Use this code file, 146-analyze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:56.320
to answer the questions on lines 4, 7, 9, and&nbsp;
11, without running the code. In the next video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.840
I will reveal the answers and recap&nbsp;
what we have covered in this topic.

